g a named woman’s account of feeling “let out of jail,” the rhetorical question invites the reader to downgrade her claimed freedom. “I ask that without judgment” calls attention to the judgment rather than removing it. The repeated military metaphors and praise for having “achieved something most people never do” also sound evaluative, as though the reader’s current arrangement is being graded before being declared insufficient.\n\n**Initial suspicion (speculative):** framing — The two-case comparison places the narrator in the role of adjudicating reported outcomes rather than standing beside the reader against the underlying trap.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Overall voice-effect assessment:** The largest gap is at the assigned instruction: “chosen threshold,” “compensation plan for this method,” and “qualified clinical instruction” turn the chapter’s main moment of relief into compliance language. The core promise is present, and the mantras themselves are generally strong, but the reader is locally made to feel administered rather than simply and confidently reassured."},"sequence_number":979}

